			<div class="content" id="post-content-4356421">
									

<p>Hi, thanks for the review! I can attempt to address some of your concerns with the piece here.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Open</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Close</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>The conprocs don't make sense, there's a ban on electronics and metals, but there's no clear reason why this is the case. The masks, where are they stored? Separately? Why is a prismatic shaft required, what would be wrong with a viewing window?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So, the reasoning behind the ban on electronics is that SCP-4064 communicates via electronic impulses, and since their 'abilities' aren't fully understood, the Foundation wants to limit their ability to spread communication as effectively as possible.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Makes me hurt… you're throwing in sciencey sounding words to add weight but you're using them wrong.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The aforementioned explanation is also the reasoning for the ban on ferromagnetic metals, which are actually a fairly common term used to describe metals like iron, nickel and cobalt, which are able to produce a magnetic charge. Magnetic charges are inherently related to electronic charges, hence the association.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Breaks suspension that SCP would do this, either the dr. wouldn't be punished in the first place (suspended pending review, for example) or the reprimand would stick (SCP doesn't care if it was cause an anomaly got you, it just means you f*ked up to let it happen).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The Foundation can be interpreted in a lot of ways, but in almost all of them, it is represented as cold and unfeeling. Cold and unfeeling, however, doesn't mean cruel. Dr. Rouslin clearly broke protocol, and was reprimanded as such, but after more info on the SCP was discovered, they removed it from his record. This is, at least in my eyes, fairly normal sounding, and non-contradictory.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>That's everything that wasn't strictly opinion based, and I hope it offered some helpful insight as to the choices I made when writing this. Thanks again for the review!</p>

